    <div id="introduction">
      <h2>1. Introduction</h2>
      <p>
        In an increasingly interconnected world, crises rarely stay contained. A drought in one region triggers food insecurity in another. A conflict displaces millions across borders. A pandemic disrupts supply chains globally.
      </p>
      <p>
        To understand these cascading emergencies, analysts, governments, and humanitarian organizations rely on <strong>crisis indices</strong>—systematic frameworks that measure, track, and compare the severity of crises across countries and over time.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> A crisis index is not just a number. It is a lens through which decision-makers see the world.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="crisis-index-definition">
      <h2>2. What is a Crisis Index?</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>crisis index</strong> is a quantitative tool that aggregates multiple indicators—such as conflict events, food insecurity, displacement, disease outbreaks, and economic stress—into a single, comparable metric.
      </p>
      <p>
        Think of it as a <strong>"thermometer for global emergencies."</strong> Just as a thermometer measures temperature, a crisis index measures the severity of a humanitarian or geopolitical crisis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Crisis indices serve three primary functions:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <li><strong>Measurement:</strong> Quantify crisis severity consistently across countries.</li>
        <li><strong>Comparison:</strong> Rank crises to prioritize resource allocation.</li>
        <li><strong>Tracking:</strong> Monitor changes over time to detect escalation or improvement.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="info-box">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> A crisis index is not reality. It is a model of reality. The best indices are transparent, data-driven, and updated frequently.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="global-crisis-index">
      <h2>3. What is a Global Crisis Index?</h2>
      <p>
        A <strong>Global Crisis Index (GCI)</strong> applies the crisis index framework to the entire planet. Instead of focusing on a single country or region, it measures crisis severity across all countries simultaneously.
      </p>
      <p>
        The goal of a global crisis index is to answer a single, urgent question: <strong>Where in the world is the situation most critical right now?</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        Unlike regional or thematic indices (e.g., a food security index or a conflict tracker), a global crisis index integrates multiple dimensions of crisis into a unified score. This allows for:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <li><strong>Cross-domain comparison:</strong> Compare a famine in one country to a conflict in another.</li>
        <li><strong>Polycrisis detection:</strong> Identify where multiple crises overlap and compound.</li>
        <li><strong>Early warning:</strong> Detect statistical anomalies that may indicate emerging crises.</li>
        <li><strong>Resource allocation:</strong> Direct humanitarian aid to the most urgent emergencies.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="info-box">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> A global crisis index is a tool for <strong>triaging the world</strong>. It tells decision-makers where to look first.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="how-it-works">
      <h2>4. How Does a Crisis Index Work?</h2>
      <p>
        Most crisis indices follow a similar architecture. They collect data from multiple sources, normalize it, and combine it into a composite score.
      </p>

      <h3>4.1 Data Sources</h3>
      <p>
        Crisis indices rely on publicly available, authoritative data sources:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <li><strong>Conflict:</strong> ACLED, GDELT, UCDP</li>
        <li><strong>Food Security:</strong> IPC, FEWS NET, WFP</li>
        <li><strong>Health:</strong> WHO, CDC, OpenDisease</li>
        <li><strong>Displacement:</strong> UNHCR, IDMC</li>
        <li><strong>Climate:</strong> USGS, NASA, Open-Meteo, GDACS</li>
        <li><strong>Economic:</strong> World Bank, IMF</li>
        <li><strong>Humanitarian Access:</strong> ReliefWeb, OCHA</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>4.2 Scoring Methodology</h3>
      <p>
        A crisis index typically uses a <strong>weighted composite model</strong>. Each dimension (e.g., conflict, food security) is scored separately, then combined into a single overall score. Weights reflect the relative importance of each dimension to overall crisis severity.
      </p>
