      </div>

      <h3>4.3 Update Frequency</h3>
      <p>
        The most valuable crisis indices update <strong>frequently</strong>—often daily or even every 5 minutes. Traditional indices (like the UN's INFORM) update monthly, which means they can miss sudden escalations.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> Speed is a competitive advantage. A real-time crisis index is more useful than a monthly one, even if the methodology is simpler.
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="why-crisis-indices-matter">
      <h2>5. Why Crisis Indices Matter</h2>
      <p>
        Crisis indices are not academic exercises. They have real-world consequences:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <li><strong>Humanitarian aid:</strong> Organizations use indices to decide where to deploy resources.</li>
        <li><strong>Government policy:</strong> Governments use indices to inform foreign policy and defense decisions.</li>
        <li><strong>Media attention:</strong> Journalists use indices to identify underreported crises.</li>
        <li><strong>Financial markets:</strong> Investors use indices to assess geopolitical and climate risk.</li>
        <li><strong>Public awareness:</strong> Citizens use indices to understand the state of the world.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="info-box">
        <strong>Key insight:</strong> A crisis index is a <strong>decision-support tool</strong>. Its value is measured not by its precision, but by its impact on action.
      </div>
    </div>

