    <div id="history">
      <h2>8. History of Crisis Indices</h2>
      <p>
        The concept of a crisis index is relatively new. While governments have tracked crises for centuries, the first systematic global crisis index emerged in the early 2000s with the <strong>INFORM Severity Index</strong> (2014), developed by a technical working group under the UN-convened INFORM initiative.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since then, several indices have emerged, each with a different focus:
      </p>
      <ul style="padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
        <li><strong>INFORM Severity Index (2014):</strong> UN-convened global severity score.</li>
        <li><strong>Fragile States Index (2005):</strong> Annual measure of state fragility.</li>
        <li><strong>World Risk Report (2011):</strong> Annual disaster risk index.</li>
        <li><strong>GCIS Fusion (2026):</strong> Real-time, API-driven crisis intelligence.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The shift from <strong>static annual or monthly indices to real-time, API-driven systems</strong> represents a fundamental transformation in how crisis intelligence is delivered—from historical reporting to live awareness.
      </p>
